-## Open Design Choice: Concurrent Visit Edits
+## Decisions & Alternatives
 
-OPEN: select the concurrency policy before completing this node's public contract.
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Concurrent-edit policy | Whole-visit revisions: each submission names its base revision; competing revisions go to coordinator review | Field-aware three-way merge: disjoint field edits combine automatically; overlapping edits go to review | A larger coordinator review queue is preferred over merge rules that could silently combine scientifically related edits. The server cannot infer field-level scientific relationships, and an incorrect automatic merge would be harder to detect than an unresolved queue item. |
+| Initial `base_revision_id` for new visits | `null` | Any sentinel string or missing field | Null is the only value that cannot be confused with a real revision_id. An absent field would require a separate code path; `null` unifies the check for "no prior revision named." |
+| Coordinator resolution action | Select from existing revisions | Allow coordinator to author a new reconciled payload | For the first release, selecting from existing submissions keeps the operation simple and ensures every resolved record is traceable to a submitted observation. Coordinator-authored payloads are deferred. |
 
-Two approaches remain credible:
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
 
-- Whole-visit revisions: each edit names its starting revision. Concurrent edits produce competing complete revisions for a coordinator to resolve. This preserves the worker's submitted visit as a unit and needs relatively little merge logic, but even edits to unrelated fields create review work. Tablet-sync would retain base revision IDs and display pending competing revisions. Review-export would need a revision comparison workflow and exclude unresolved visits from its export set.
-- Field-aware three-way merge: each edit supplies its base state and changes. Disjoint field changes combine automatically; overlapping changes require review. This reduces review volume and gets more visits ready for export, but requires maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries. Automatically combined fields can also cross scientific relationships the rules do not understand. Tablet-sync would retain base snapshots and send structured changes. Review-export would need field-level provenance and a review view for overlapping changes.
+### Resolved
+1. ✅ Concurrent-edit policy: whole-visit revisions (not field-aware three-way merge).
 
-The choice trades review workload and submission size against implementation burden and preservation of whole-visit intent. Neither approach may discard original submissions. Field-aware merging is not ruled out by a requirement that every combined record receive manual approval.
+### Deferred
+1. Concurrent coordinator resolution: if two coordinators resolve the same conflict simultaneously with different selections, last write wins under the current design. A locking or compare-and-swap guard may be warranted if coordinators are likely to work the same queue simultaneously.
+2. Coordinator rejection: whether a coordinator can mark a visit as rejected outright (neither competing revision is field-correct) and what state that produces.
+3. Limits on competing revisions: no per-visit cap is defined. If many workers submit against stale bases, the review queue grows without bound.
+4. Post-submission notification to tablets: after a clean submission, the accepting tablet receives the new head in the acceptance response. Competing submitters receive `conflicted` in their acceptance response, but there is no push notification to tablets that submitted earlier and now have a conflict against their accepted revision.
 
